```markdown
# AI Tidy Tabs 🧹🧠

[![License: MIT](https://img.shields.io/badge/License-MIT-yellow.svg)](https://opensource.org/licenses/MIT)
[![Contributions Welcome](https://img.shields.io/badge/contributions-welcome-brightgreen.svg?style=flat)](https://github.com/blood0jade-bot/ai-tidy-tabs/issues)

> An intelligent browser tool that uses AI to automatically organize, group, and clean up messy browser tabs.

## 🌟 Features

* **Smart Grouping:** Uses AI to analyze the content and titles of your open tabs, automatically categorizing them into logical, color-coded Tab Groups (e.g., "Work," "Research," "Social").
* **Duplicate Detection:** Identifies and securely closes redundant or duplicate tabs to free up system memory.
* **Auto-Tidy Mode:** Set a custom threshold and let the AI automatically consolidate your workspace when things get too cluttered.
* **Privacy First:** Tab data is processed securely, ensuring your browsing habits remain private.

## 🚀 Installation

1. Clone the repository:
   ```bash
   git clone [https://github.com/blood0jade-bot/ai-tidy-tabs.git](https://github.com/blood0jade-bot/ai-tidy-tabs.git)

```

2. Navigate to the project directory:
```bash
cd ai-tidy-tabs

```


3. Install dependencies:
```bash
npm install

```


4. Build the project:
```bash
npm run build

```


5. **Load into your browser:**
* **Chrome/Edge:** Go to `chrome://extensions/`, enable **Developer mode**, click **Load unpacked**, and select the `dist` folder from this repository.
* **Firefox:** Go to `about:debugging#/runtime/this-firefox`, click **Load Temporary Add-on**, and select the `manifest.json` file.



## ⚙️ Configuration

To enable the AI categorization features, configure your AI provider:

1. Click on the extension icon in your browser toolbar.
2. Open the **Settings** menu.
3. Enter your preferred AI provider API key (e.g., OpenAI, Gemini) and save.

## 📖 Usage

* **Manual Tidy:** Click the extension icon and press **"Tidy Now"** to instantly organize your current window.
* **Context Search:** Use the search bar within the extension to find specific tabs based on context rather than relying on exact title matches.
* **Custom Rules:** Set specific domains to bypass the AI and always group together (e.g., force all `github.com` tabs into a "Development" group).

